.TH CLOG_FLUSH 3 "2026-08-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_flush \- block until queued log records are written
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

void clog_flush(clog logger);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks the calling thread until write(2) has accepted every record that
was in the queue of logger (or of a sibling handle on the same target)
when the call started, together with whatever sits in a partly filled
aggregation buffer.

On a synchronous logger, one created with no clog_async_cfg_t (see
clog_create_from_fd_mp(3) and clog_create_from_file_mp(3)), the call
does nothing and returns at once.

A record that another thread submits at the same time as this call, or
after it, is not guaranteed to be covered.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
You can call this on any open handle at any time, even while an
ordinary clog_<level>() call runs on the same handle or on a sibling
handle.
On a rare allocation failure the async queue can refuse the flush
request, and the function then returns at once instead of waiting for
an acknowledgment that would never come.
In a forked child, a logger whose async mode has fallen back to
synchronous logging (see clog_create_from_fd_mp(3)) does nothing here,
exactly like a synchronous logger.
When a write stops early with an error that a later attempt can get
past, such as ENOSPC, the bytes it did not take stay in the aggregation
buffer and the next flush offers them again, so this call can return
while part of what it flushed is still pending.  After any other write
error, such as EPIPE, the flush drops those bytes and writes a record
that names the loss.  clog_create_from_fd_mp(3) describes both rules and
what they promise about the framing of a record.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_create_from_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_create_from_file_mp (3),
.BR clog_close (3)
